dr. King's legacy is one of action its  action motivated by courage and most of  all by love he was a man of great faith  and he believed not just in the ideals  of the country but also the spirit of  people to make those ideals a reality he  more than anyone knew how hard the task  was he had set himself trying to change  the culture of a country that was based  on not just slavery but inequality that  had been baked into law this was a heavy  lift  he had tremendous faith that he could do  it we can look at that today at the  problems we're facing and see that no  matter how insurmountable they seem you  can start with one action and people  will join you people of goodwill and  faith will come together and they are  coming together to continue this legacy  the fight for justice and equality is an  ongoing one and King knew that he was  looking at history but he was also  looking ahead to what the country could  be but he knew that it was going to take  effort by every one of us and it was  going to take effort every day to make  the ideals of America a reality and to  keep them a reality when you look back  at the history of this country you see  tremendous progress and we have done  some wonderful things but we've also had  some tremendous backlash to some of the  best things that have ever happened to  us  going back centuries King knew that and  he saw that most importantly he'd lived  it he lived in the south he knew that  reality he knew the good people there  were often overwhelmed by people who  were clinging to history clinging to the  Past clinging frankly to a stale sterile  way of life that they thought was  benefiting them it hasn't changed  you know people are still stuck in that  mindset of thinking that things used to  be better they could only get back to a  time when things were stable and in  their view the right people were in  charge but King knew that that was a  time that really didn't benefit anyone  that we're all better when everyone  advances he also knew that this was hard  it was going to be hard work and it's  got to be ongoing work when King talked  about the other America he was talking  mostly about the racial divide but I  think we're still in that situation  today where we have people who too  easily let things divide us we're  divided by  we're divided economically we're still  divided racially in some very very deep  and fundamental ways in which we see the  problems of this country and King talked  about the need to step back from that  and to find the commonality that we all  share and come together I was born in  Greensboro and when I was very young the  students sit-in movement started at A&amp;T  University there while I was too young  to remember that you know my parents  remember the students and the civic  leaders who were organizing that meeting  in the basement of my dad's church and  one other church in Greensboro King came  to North Carolina several times in the  early 60s to encourage that movement  because he knew that young people were  the heart of it they were the spirit of  it and they were going to pick up the  legacy that he was laying down and carry  it forward my dad was born in the 1930s  in rural North Carolina and when you  think about that time period you tend to  think about you know the beauty of  Eastern North Carolina and sort of a  peaceful life but under the surface it  was a very difficult time for minorities  for blacks in the south this is before  Miranda was even a case much less rights  that are established for all of us it  was before so many of the court cases  that established the due process that we  take for granted and so when you if you  were a black person in the 1930s Eastern  North Carolina on the wrong side of the  law the chance was very very good that  you weren't going to get the kind of due  process injustice that we all hope to  count on today that we hope is available  to everyone today and so oftentimes for  people caught in that situation the only  remedy was just to leave the area and my  grandfather would help them make that  change that's a very important lesson  that I learned from both my grandfather  and my father that if people can't count  on fundamental fairness within our  systems then they're lost they feel  outside of those structures and they  feel that they have nowhere to turn it's  been very important to me as a  prosecutor and as someone who's worked  within government to make sure that the  government that we have is one that  looks at everybody equally and treats  everyone with fundamental fairness  we're commemorating the 50th anniversary  of the death of dr. King this year and  those were difficult times and I  remember that event because it was  really one of the first events of  national significance that impacted me  as a child I was eight years old and I  remember that day in April I was driving  down the highway with my mother my  mother and had brought us kids to the  airport to drop my dad off for flight we  were riding home and the radio was  playing the usual music news of the day  and they broke in with an announcement  that dr. King had been shot and had died  and my brothers and I didn't really  understand the significance of that at  the time and my mother's reaction was  what struck us as British is driving  down the highway and she slowed down and  she just was so affected by it and I  remember that she pulled over to the  side of the road and she put her head  down on the steering wheel and she began  to cry and my brothers and I did not  know what to do to comfort her we did  not know what had happened and finally  she said to us you know dr. King has  been killed and she explained what that  meant and it was literally it was  literally as if the very air around us  had changed you know you have that  feeling of something that is of such  great importance when my dad came back  from the trip he told us that the pilot  had come on board with an announcement  that dr. King had died and on the plane  a number of people had begun to cry but  that some people in the plane had  actually clapped and we could not  comprehend this my brothers and I could  not comprehend how someone could clap  over the death of anyone and that was  the first time that my parents sat down  and had to explain to us about racism in  America and how everyone did not believe  in equality of all people and did not  support dr. King's goals and it was it  was as you can imagine heart-rending in  so many ways King's legacy is one of  great strength and great commitment we  look at all the wonderful things he was  able to accomplish I think that he  inspires so many people today because at  the time no one knew whether it was  going to work or not I mean  it's against changing the law changing  culture changing society are always high  so you look at his courage you look at  his perseverance you look at the deep  faith then he brought to this he was  first and foremost a man of deep and  fundamental faith and you see how he  inspired ordinary people to come  together and with the force of their  commitment literally change this country  at the same time you see people who were  not supporters of him at that time  obviously leading up to the events  leading up to his death change is  frightening for people people who  thought that they had a particular place  in society that they should be theirs  forever and someone comes along and says  no actually everyone deserves this place  at the table everyone is part of the  Beloved Community and you see the  lengths that people will go to to  protect their position - to fight  against change you see the lengths that  fear will take people two people were  fundamentally afraid of King's message  and what it might mean for losses in  their own lives and I think that's  that's a lesson for today as we look at  conflict and how to manage it and how  people react to the changes that we see  in our country over the years and even  now the way that people will cling to  their fears as opposed to opening their  hearts up to possibilities is something  that can never be discounted King's  death is a reminder of that but the  commitment and the perseverance and  strength of people ordinary people  working people young people who  literally change this country is also  something that we can't forget and we  can draw strength from it we see it we  see it now with the young people leading  all the movements black lives matter me  to the Parkland students the way that  they have come together and found their  voice and shared it with all of us and  that they're literally shifting the air  in this country now around these very  important issues is a legacy of King and  the civil rights movement we've made  great progress in this country and I'm  optimistic about America I always am I  look back at all the things we've been  able to achieve since the birth of this  country and I still think that our great  days  ahead of us and we can build on those we  can continue to advance I think that we  we always however have backlash whenever  we have great progress you will see the  pendulum swing back in many ways you'll  see fear taking over you'll see that  distrust of change taking over but then  you know people who are affected by that  will realize their own strength and they  will push the needle forward again I  think the people that we're seeing now  emerge as student leaders in 50 years  we're gonna be reviewing them and  looking back at how they have brought  this country further along the road to  equality and I'm looking forward to that  I can't wait to see what these young  people are doing
 

